{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":259688,"duration_api_ms":257352,"num_turns":54,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"cc989ad3-7e61-48cb-afb6-f697e3820c44","total_cost_usd":2.1091935,"usage":{"input_tokens":37,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89866,"cache_read_input_tokens":2705292,"output_tokens":7788,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89866,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":37,"outputTokens":7788,"cacheReadInputTokens":2705292,"cacheCreationInputTokens":89866,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.1091935,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"Fix inconsistent SUB_ADULT_FEEDING_WEIGHT constant: 0.65 in crates/tank_core/src/types/biology.rs:148 vs 0.67 in crates/tank_core/src/systems/shrimp.rs:16. These two private constants serve the same conceptual role (sub-adult feeding demand relative to adults) but disagree on the value. biology.rs uses it in AnimalState::feeding_units() and add_reserve_by_feeding_units(), while shrimp.rs uses it in the actual daily feeding/routing/condition code. Consolidate to a single shared constant (or single source of truth) to prevent silent divergence in feeding weight calculations between the two modules.","rationale":"A sub-adult with 10 shrimp gets feeding weight 6.5 vs 6.7 depending on which code path runs. While the biology.rs methods are currently dead code (feeding_units() and add_reserve_by_feeding_units() have zero call sites), they appear intended for use and will silently produce different results when called. This is a correctness bug waiting to happen."},{"body":"Fix failing test trim_plants_is_closed_system_via_helpers in crates/tank_core/tests/budget_helpers.rs:242-267. Two issues: (1) Line 253 calls assert_n_conserved() but TrimPlantsAndRemove intentionally exports biomass from the tank — this is no longer a closed-system operation, so the nitrogen conservation assertion is incorrect for the renamed action semantics. (2) Line 259 looks up budget label \"action:trim_plants\" but the action was renamed to TrimPlantsAndRemove with budget label \"action:trim_plants_and_remove\" (engine.rs:791), so the lookup always returns None and the test would panic at the .expect() even if the conservation check passed.","rationale":"This is a real test failure visible in `cargo test` right now. The test was not updated to match the TrimPlants → TrimPlantsAndRemove rename and the associated change in mass-balance semantics (remove = export from tank, not redistribute within tank)."},{"body":"Fix incorrect reserve fraction in RemoveShrimp action at crates/tank_core/src/engine.rs:684. The code computes `removed_frac = count.min(total) / total` where `total = state.animal.total_count()` (adults + sub-adults + juveniles) but `count` is the number of adults to remove. This dilutes the reserve fraction — removing 5 adults from a population of 5 adults + 10 juveniles only removes 5/15 = 33% of adult reserve instead of 100%. The denominator should be `state.animal.adult.count` to correctly export the proportional adult reserve.","rationale":"When a user removes all adult shrimp, one-third of the adult reserve stays in the tank as orphaned mass that can never be consumed (no adults left to use it). This is a mass-balance correctness bug introduced during the flat→staged AnimalState migration."},{"body":"Remove dead code in crates/tank_core/src/types/biology.rs: the methods feeding_units() (line 441), add_reserve_by_feeding_units() (line 477), try_spend_reserve_g() (line 497), and transfer_dead_reserve_g() (line 517) have zero call sites across the entire codebase. They were likely intended as refactoring targets but the shrimp.rs code does the same operations inline with its own (different) constant values. Either wire these methods into the actual simulation code or remove them to avoid confusion with the divergent constants.","rationale":"Dead code with different constants (0.65 vs 0.67 for SUB_ADULT_FEEDING_WEIGHT) is a maintainability risk. Future developers may call these methods assuming they match the simulation behavior, introducing subtle bugs."},{"body":"The migrate_v5_to_v6 function at crates/tank_core/src/save.rs:573-576 is a no-op that relies entirely on #[serde(default)] for new fields. While the post-migration fixup at line 204-206 populates the habitat_registry, it does not set geometry.hardscape_area_cm2 or filter.media_area_cm2 to their intended defaults (0.0 and 2000.0 respectively). Since #[serde(default)] on hardscape_area_cm2 produces 0.0 (correct) and filter.media_area_cm2 uses a custom default function returning 2000.0 (correct), this actually works — but only because serde defaults happen to match. Document this dependency explicitly in the migration comment or set the values explicitly for robustness.","rationale":"If someone later changes the serde default for media_area_cm2 without updating migrations, old saves would silently get the new default instead of the value that was correct at the time of migration. This is minor but worth noting for a scientific simulation that values reproducibility."}],"summary":"Reviewed the diff spanning 42 files (+1507/-367 lines) covering: provenance schema (ParamMeta with deny_unknown_fields, param_meta key validation), habitat registry system, three-stage shrimp lifecycle (juvenile/sub-adult/adult), snapshot field renaming to explicit units, element-to-ion conversion module, schema v5→v6 migration, and TUI updates. Found 3 real bugs (inconsistent SUB_ADULT_FEEDING_WEIGHT constants 0.65 vs 0.67, failing test from TrimPlants rename not propagated to budget_helpers test, incorrect reserve fraction denominator in RemoveShrimp), 1 dead code block with divergent constants, and 1 migration robustness concern. The provenance schema itself is well-designed with proper deny_unknown_fields, optional fields, Display impl including notes, and range-warning-not-error semantics. The habitat registry computation is clean with proper clamping. The core acceptance criteria for provenance are met."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"b938ff54-7428-43c0-a08b-cec93b607aa7"}
